  Version 0.1.31 (2026-11-03)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * ORDER 373: the records dpkg-stereo 0.1.28 and older lost come back. Those versions counted a package as installed
    only in dpkg's state "installed" (0.1.29 fixed that, ORDER 367): a run that left one with its trigger pending, as
    apt's --no-triggers runs leave systemd whenever a package ships a file under /usr/lib/systemd/catalog, dropped its
    records, and the next projection left its lean files as "changed since its package installed it" (a lean conffile
    as "changed by the person"). --verify did not name them, --restore could not bring their originals back, and dpkg
    would ask about such a conffile at its package's next upgrade. On the station 378 rows were so (systemd 313, udev
    50), and /etc/default/nss was a lean conffile called the person's.
  * A one-time migration (marker orphaned-lean-v1), run by the post-invoke hook at the end of the dpkg run that installs
    this version (outside the postinst, where dpkg's run is half done, as the conffile owners' migration of 0.1.22) and
    then by the hooks until it completes, judges each such file of an installed package against its package's own file at the installed version. dpkg's database
    names the version (a left row names none), and an original is that version's only when its MD5 is the one dpkg
    records for the file (md5sums, or the conffile's hash). The original comes from the pristine store (a conffile's
    copy, found by that MD5), then as restore takes it: APT's cache, a configured source, and snapshot.debian.org only
    for the person's --repair and --repair-consumers, never inside dpkg's hooks.
      - exactly the projection of that original, checked again by its reader: its record comes back (a conffile's with
        its pristine copies, so pre-invoke puts it back before dpkg compares it) and its row goes;
      - anything else stays the person's, as before; a file without the pointer line every projection carries is not
        one, and its original is not fetched to say so;
      - a file in its package's own bytes (a stale row) is examined again;
      - a projection today's check refuses (an older engine's) goes back to its package's bytes, its row saying why.
  * What came back and what stayed the person's is said once, in the system's language with English beside it. A file
    whose original is nowhere at hand keeps its row; --verify names it (UNRECORDED; exit 2, the check is not complete),
    --status counts it, and the step is kept as a problem naming each package version, why, and what gives the records
    back (sudo dpkg-stereo --repair, which also asks snapshot.debian.org; or the package's next upgrade). A dpkg run
    tries a version again only when its installed version, APT's lists or APT's cache changed. --restore (and so
    dpkg-stereo's own removal) names such files too, version by version, as staying lean, with why and what brings
    them back.
  * Counted read-only on the station (dpkg-stereo 0.1.16; 401 such rows of installed packages): 375 files get their
    record back (systemd 313, udev 50, libc-bin 3, man-db 2, cracklib-runtime 2, initramfs-tools 2, install-info,
    mailcap and menu 1 each); 19 stay the person's, none of them carrying the pointer line (systemd's sleep.conf,
    system.conf and user.conf among them); 3 rows were files in their package's own bytes (nis, libcufile-13-4,
    media-types); 4 conffiles of nut, readable by root only, were not read. cracklib-runtime 2.9.6-5.3+b1 is in no
    configured source any more, so its 2 files wait for the person's --repair.
  * A removed package's lean conffiles go back to its package's bytes. dpkg keeps a removed package's conffiles, and
    their hashes, until a purge, and an install of the package again compares each with that hash: a lean one looked
    changed by the person, so the install kept it lean and --verify called it EDITED (ORDER 367). Post-invoke now writes
    back the pristine original of each recorded conffile that is still exactly its projection, of a package dpkg lists
    in "config-files" with that original's hash, and that today's renderer makes byte for byte from that original:
    hashes alone would trust a record whose lean hash is wrong and overwrite a person's edit (ORDER 376); a file the
    renderer does not make stays as it is and is said, and installing the package again will ask about it. Installing
    the package again is quiet, finds the conffile unmodified, and projects and records it again. What the person sees: after removing a package (not purging it), its settings files
    in /etc are as the package shipped them, comments included, until it is installed again; a settings file the person
    changed stays as they made it; a purge removes them all, as before.
  * restore's order for an original (APT's cache, a configured source, snapshot.debian.org) is one function, which
    restore and the migration share.
  * Tests: test/orphans-test.sh. 0.1.28 loses systemd's and three fixtures' records through one `dpkg --no-triggers -i`
    (ORDER 367's probe) and their conffiles' through one `dpkg --no-triggers -r` (pre-invoke puts no conffile back for a
    removal); the package under test is then installed over it. On 0.1.29 it fails (every control passes, so the
    loss is reproduced, and the checks of what comes back fail); on 0.1.31 it passes.

