-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.29 (2026-11-03)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * ORDER 367: a dpkg run that changes nothing costs the hooks almost nothing. On the A10 (run aba2-20261103)
    `dpkg --configure -a` with nothing to do took 1.4 to 1.8 s lean against 1.0 s with nothing projected. In the
    lane's container (Debian testing, 3,856 packages, 1,302 record tables, 53,500 records, 105 projected conffiles,
    a Ryzen 5 5600G) dpkg alone took 65 ms, and 0.1.28's two hooks 368 ms more; 0.1.29's take 64 ms (median of 10;
    of 20 alternating runs: 380 against 115 ms, and 649 against 171 ms on a busier machine). Where it went: each hook
    compiled the 2,900-line program (28 ms) and imported modules it rarely needs (64 ms: concurrent.futures alone
    brings logging, traceback and _colorize); pre-invoke put every lean conffile back in its package's bytes for every
    run and post-invoke projected each again, reading their packages' record tables; post-invoke parsed dpkg's
    whole database, looked at every package's file list and read every recorded conffile.
  * The program is /usr/lib/dpkg-stereo/dpkg_stereo.py, byte-compiled by the postinst (py3compile, removed by the
    prerm's py3clean, as Debian's Python packages are), and /usr/sbin/dpkg-stereo loads it; a module is imported
    when it is first needed; the hooks parse no options (dpkg.cfg.d runs them without any); the problems engine is
    loaded only when the step has a kept record to clear (its clear does nothing when the record's file is not
    there).
  * A configure run compares the conffiles only of the packages it configures from "unpacked" (src/main/
    configure.c, deferred_configure), so pre-invoke gives dpkg back only the conffiles of packages its database
    does not show installed; an unpack or an install compares those of the packages it unpacks, which no hook is
    told, so every lean one goes back, as before; a removal or a purge compares none (src/main/remove.c), so
    nothing goes back.
  * packages.tsv's second header line keeps the digest of dpkg's database (the bytes of its status file and
    journal: dpkg writes the status file again at the end of every run, so its time says nothing), the time of
    dpkg's info directory (dpkg writes every file list through a rename there, so a reinstall of the same version,
    which leaves the database's bytes as they were, changes it) and the digest of the table's rows. A run that
    finds all three as they were, with neither the directory nor any row in the second the table was written in
    (git's racy rule, as before), reads no field of dpkg's database and no file list: no package changed.
  * A conffile is read for its lean form, or for its package's bytes, only when its size is the one looked for.
  * Found while measuring: dpkg-stereo counted a package as installed only in dpkg's state "installed". A run with
    --no-triggers, as each of apt's dpkg runs is, leaves a package whose file trigger it activated in
    "triggers-pending" until a later run processes it (apt leaves systemd so whenever a package ships a file in
    /usr/lib/systemd/catalog, binfmt.d or sysctl.d); post-invoke took it for removed and dropped its records, and
    its lean files stayed lean and unrecorded, called "changed since its package installed it" by the next
    projection, so --verify did not name them and --restore could not bring them back (313 of systemd's 320
    projected files after one `dpkg --no-triggers -i` on the lane's image). A package another one's unpack
    deconfigures went the same way through "half-configured". installed, triggers-pending, triggers-awaited and
    half-configured now count (dpkg(1), "Package states"), and the lean toolkit's reader of lc1 marks
    (lean/package.py, installed) counts the same states: it took only "install ok installed", so a marked package
    on hold, or with its trigger pending, was taken for an unmarked one (the renderer's identity does not move).
  * A run that changes a package reads dpkg's database once (it read it three times: post-invoke, the projection
    and the package table each). In the same container, one package of 28 files installed, upgraded, removed and
    purged, medians of 20: 1,326, 1,276, 905 and 982 ms with 0.1.28; 970, 982, 606 and 616 ms now.
  * Tests: test/hook-cost-test.sh (red on 0.1.28: every projected conffile written again by a run that changed
    nothing; four mutants of 0.1.29 fail its other cases: no info directory check, no conffile back for a
    configure run, no size match, no clear of a kept problem) and test/trigger-states-test.sh (red on 0.1.28: 8
    of 18).

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.28 (2026-11-03)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * ORDER 365: --restore brings back every original, fetching the exact version itself. On the A10 (run
    aba2-20261103) 0.1.24 left 1,597 files lean in 33 packages whose installed version was neither in
    APT's cache nor in any configured source, and advised `apt-get install --reinstall NAME`, which can
    only fetch a version a source offers. Now an original without a pristine copy comes from APT's cache
    (that architecture's .deb, by APT's own name), then the configured sources (apt-get download, as
    before), then snapshot.debian.org, the Debian Project's archive of every version Debian has published,
    through its machine-readable interface (/mr/binary/NAME/VERSION/binfiles?fileinfo=1 names the .deb by
    its SHA-1, /file/SHA1 is the file), downloaded with APT's own apt-helper (APT's proxies and TLS). A
    fetched .deb waits in APT's partial/ directory; its SHA-1, its control file's Package, Version and
    Architecture and every file it gives back (against the record's sha_orig) are checked, and only then
    does it go into APT's cache under APT's own name (NAME_VERSION_ARCH.deb, an epoch's colon as %3a).
  * Snapshot is asked for a package APT's own data call Debian's: the Origin of the Release file of each
    source that lists the version, or other versions of the package, with the installed version's
    Maintainer (dpkg's database) deciding where both Debian and another archive list it; and for a package
    no list holds any more, once, by its exact name, version and architecture. A package of another
    archive (deb-multimedia, a vendor) is not asked of snapshot. Once snapshot cannot be reached, it is not
    asked again in that run. The person's --restore, --hold and --repair and dpkg-stereo's own removal
    fetch; the hooks' migrations do not (the network in dpkg's hooks stays as it was).
  * What stays lean is said package version by package version, in the nine languages, with why (no
    network, not on snapshot, another archive, a refused file, a .deb with other files, APT's own error)
    and the one thing that brings it back; apt-get's reinstall is named only for a version a configured
    source offers, and only once dpkg-stereo is removed. The restore problem keeps each package version
    (dpkg-stereo --problems lists them); a restore of some packages keeps the others an earlier restore
    kept while their files stay lean.
  * --restore [PACKAGE...] --deb FILE.deb: a .deb the person hands restore (a vendor's, such as Zoom's,
    which no archive keeps) is taken only when its control file is exactly an installed version with
    recorded files and every file it gives back is the recorded original; otherwise nothing is taken from
    it, and why is said.
  * Depends on apt: the fetch uses APT's apt-helper (before, apt-get and apt-cache were used undeclared).
  * A Multi-Arch: same package's .deb is read for its own architecture only: the twin's .deb of the other
    architecture in APT's cache was taken for it before (any NAME_VERSION_* name matched).
  * The post-invoke hook gives the same records whichever second dpkg and the hook ran in (found by Opus in
    ORDER 363's proofs: two runs of one case ended with different --verify output). A package whose file
    list was written in the second packages.tsv was written in is looked at again (git's racy rule); then a
    file the person changed after its projection lost its record, so --verify passed silently, and a
    Multi-Arch: same instance's shared files were examined only when the twin holding their record looked
    changed too, so a script dpkg put back in its package's bytes after a reinstall could stay so. Now a
    file whose bytes are neither its projection nor dpkg's (its md5sums or conffile hash) keeps its record,
    and --verify names it as changed after its projection; a changed instance's examination reads the
    records its twin holds for the paths its file list shares; and a path recorded for this version is never
    also "left" for the other instance (a person's edit of a shared conffile is named once, as CHANGED).
    What a removed package left alone with its reason goes with it too (--verify named a removed instance's
    conffile as changed by the person). test/post-invoke-seconds-test.sh runs each case with the instance's
    row made racy and not.
  * Tests: test/restore-fetch-test.sh (a local server with snapshot's interface; no internet). The three
    tests that failed with 0.1.26 too are fixed: no-fallbacks-test.sh names the unpacked program's own
    journal catalogs (the problems engine forgets the problems of a package dpkg does not list and whose
    catalog it does not find); migrate-test.sh tells a full projection from the one-time migration line
    0.1.25 added; concurrent-test.sh case D says it needs dpkg-stereo 0.1.4 or older.
  * Credits: snapshot.debian.org (the Debian Project; hardware and hosting by Glasklar Teknik AB, LeaseWeb
    Netherlands B.V. and the Wellcome Trust Sanger Institute; its current web frontend by Baptiste
    Beauplat); APT's apt-helper (the APT team); David Paleino's debsnap (devscripts), whose use of the
    same interface this follows.

