  Version 0.1.27 (2026-11-03)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * ORDER 363 (Daniel, 2026-11-03: "make it recognize the lc1 mark, it's ours to modify anyways"): the
    other architecture of a projected Multi-Arch: same package installs, upgrades, reinstalls and is
    removed. Debian's dpkg refused it ("trying to overwrite shared '/usr/share/gcc/python/libstdcxx/v6/
    __init__.py', which is different from other instances of package libstdc++6:i386", apt exit 100),
    so Steam and wine32 could not be installed where dpkg-stereo had projected libstdc++6. The edition's
    dpkg (1.23.11+stereo3d1+lc1) accepts a shared file that is the projection this package's record
    names when the file being unpacked is the file every other instance shipped and the recorded
    original, and refuses every other difference as Debian's dpkg does; this package pre-depends on it
    (dpkg-lean-code, the contract it provides), so apt unpacks and configures the edition's dpkg in dpkg runs
    before this package's, and the twins an apt run brings with it are unpacked by the new dpkg even with
    APT::Immediate-Configure=false (measured on a machine with systemd: with a plain Depends, apt put Debian's
    dpkg, the edition's and the twins in one dpkg run, which refused libstdc++6:i386). Shared files stay
    projected, as every other file.
  * One projection, one record, for a shared file: a run that installs or reinstalls the other
    architecture's instance reads that instance's record too, so the file our dpkg put back in the
    package's bytes is projected again from the same checked render, and its record moves to the
    instance that projected it; a file its twin projected earlier in the same run is no longer kept as
    "changed since its package installed it".
  * When dpkg removes the instance a shared file is recorded for, the file stays (another instance still
    ships it, src/main/remove.c) and so does its projection: the record goes to that instance, at the
    recorded version, with the recorded original's MD5 in its md5sums. Before, the record went and the
    file stayed lean with no record, so neither --verify nor --restore knew it.
  * Every projection records the MD5 of its original (before, only conffiles did).
  * Upgrades remove once the second records and the false left rows earlier versions kept for shared
    files (marker shared-twin-records-v1); no file changes.

