-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.25 (2026-11-02)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * ORDER 350 (Daniel, 2026-11-02: "Why machinery to keep a failure for each?"): a step that does not complete
    is kept by the edition's one engine, sparky-stereo-problems, and shown by its session notifier, whose Repair
    runs dpkg-stereo --repair; the package's own store and user units go. --problems and --repair keep working
    through the engine; --dismiss is the engine's Hide (yours, kept listed, back when the step fails again);
    --notify starts the session's notifier. What a step could not do (its items) is the record's data. The
    messages, word for word, are built into journal catalogs; the problems go when the package is removed
  * Nothing fails silently (Daniel, 2026-11-01: "either it works or not and
    the user must know"; ORDER 346b, rows U19, U20, U57 and U58 of ORDER
    343's audit). A step that cannot do what it is for says what failed, why
    and what to do, in the system's language with the English line beside
    it, and keeps it in /var/lib/dpkg-stereo/problems/STEP.json until the
    step next succeeds. New commands: --problems (exit 1 while any is kept),
    --repair (runs again what each problem names), --dismiss STEP and
    --notify (the session's side: dpkg-stereo-problems.path shows each
    problem once as a notification). The messages ship in the nine
    languages in /usr/share/sparky/dpkg-stereo.
  * dpkg's hooks still return 0: dpkg ends its run when a hook fails
    ("cannot execute hook 'exit 3', exit code 768", measured), and APT's
    update fails the whole update when its hook fails ("E: Problem executing
    scripts APT::Update::Post-Invoke-Success", exit 100, measured). The
    post-invoke and pre-invoke errors are now kept and said, where they were
    only appended to error.log; the HR hook no longer throws its output away.
  * --verify --deep never says clean when it did not check: a file whose
    package's .deb could not be had or read is NODEB with the real reason
    (apt-get download's own error, dpkg-deb's exit status, the archive
    reader's error), is not counted as checked, and the command exits 2
    (dpkg(1): an error interacting with the system) when nothing else is
    wrong. --restore, --hr and --fetch-hr name the same reasons.
  * Python bytecode: a cache that cannot be refreshed (a compile error, a
    cache that cannot be written, an interpreter that is not installed or
    that fails) is kept with each file and interpreter. A cache that could
    not be written used to end the projection before its records were
    saved; the records are now saved first.
  * The lean toolkit 0.1.23's link gate (linkcheck.py) blocks when readelf
    or nm cannot read an ELF file's inventory, naming the tool's error: a
    failed probe gave an empty inventory, so a predecessor whose nm failed
    hid a stereo import its rebuild lost. Guest firmware (resolve()'s note)
    is not read.
  * The lean toolkit 0.1.24 (ORDER 352): one label, one renderer. ORDER
    346c's Perl rule had moved the renderer's code under the label 0.1.23;
    that code is released as 0.1.24, with a table of every released identity
    (lean/identities.py) that the release gate checks each package against.
  * Zig is qualified: comments go, doc comments and zig fmt directives stay,
    and Zig's own tokenizer and zig ast-check check every projection (zig
    needed; without it a Zig file stays as authored, with that reason). A file
    another Zig file reads with @embedFile stays as authored.
  * C-family files are checked in the mode their consumer reads them in:
    assembler sources (.S, .sx) and headers for them with GCC's
    assembler-with-cpp, and a file whose only GCC refusals are directives in
    conditional groups other targets take with clang's raw lexer (clang
    needed for those files).
  * Upgrades examine once the files 0.1.24 qualifies first (marker
    renderer-migration-0124-v1): the packages owning Zig or assembler
    sources, and the files GCC or perl left as "check failed". No earlier
    projection changes form.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.24 (2026-11-01)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * The lean toolkit 0.1.23's link gate (linkcheck.py, carried here with the
    rest of the toolkit; dpkg-stereo itself does not run it) resolves an
    ELF file with its package's own Recommends installed where every
    library the file misses is shipped by a package those Recommends name
    (ORDER 311 step 4). fltk1.4-games' /usr/games/flpuzzle links
    libfltk_gl.so.1.4, libGLU.so.1 and libGL.so.1, and Debian's fltk1.4
    sends those dependencies to Recommends on purpose
    (dh_shlibdeps -pfltk1.4-games -Xflpuzzle -- -dRecommends ...flpuzzle);
    Depends-only resolution left them out, so the published
    1.4.4-4+stereo3d2+lc1 failed as a predecessor and every rebuild with
    it.
  * Why Recommends: Debian Policy 7.2 says Recommends "declares a strong,
    but not absolute, dependency" and "should list packages that would be
    found together with this one in all but unusual installations";
    dpkg-shlibdeps(1) -ddependency-field adds a program's library
    dependencies to that field ("The default dependency-field is Depends"),
    and the edition installs Recommends by default, so its systems have
    them.
  * Narrowly: only the ELF files whose every missing library such a named
    Recommends ships are resolved again, and each is printed as a note
    naming the Recommends; the previous build is treated the same way.
    Depends-only resolution stays the rule for everything else, and a
    library that no Depends and no such Recommends provides still fails.
  * dpkg-stereo --status labels the version it prints as the lean
    toolkit's ("lean 0.1.23: ... files projected"), which it is; it said
    "dpkg-stereo" before that number (ORDER 339). The renderer identity
    stays as it was: lean 0.1.23 (collapse-blank,pointer,c-drop-lines).
  * The source package carries the recipe folder as git tracks it
    (build-source.sh), so an untracked bin/__pycache__ or *.pyc in a
    working checkout no longer ships in it; outside a git work tree the
    folder is copied without __pycache__ folders and *.pyc files
    (ORDER 339).

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.23 (2026-11-01)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * The lean toolkit's link gate (linkcheck.py, carried here with the rest
    of the toolkit; dpkg-stereo itself does not run it) asks first whether
    an ELF file is the host's (ORDER 340). qemu-system-data
    (Architecture: all) ships QEMU's guest firmware in /usr/share/qemu,
    and QEMU's machine models read it with load_elf() into the guest; the
    gate ran its machine check before deciding how a file is consumed, so
    the published 1:11.1.2+ds-3+stereo3d6+lc1 failed as a predecessor
    ("ELF machine does not match amd64") and stopped the build of
    +stereo3d7. Eight files are foreign: hppa-firmware.img,
    hppa-firmware64.img (with PT_INTERP /usr/lib/pa20_64/dld.sl and a
    dynamic section), openbios-ppc, openbios-sparc32, openbios-sparc64,
    palcode-clipper, s390-ccw.img and u-boot.e500; the message showed
    five.
  * An ELF file of another machine, in an Architecture: all package, under
    /usr/share, is guest data: the gate prints it as a note and never
    traces it. That is Debian's own line: Lintian's
    binary-from-other-architecture has no package architecture to compare
    in an Architecture: all package, arch-independent-package-contains-
    binary-or-object asks for an override for "binary blobs for other
    purposes independent of the host architecture (such as BIOS updates or
    firmware)", /usr/share is for architecture-independent files
    (arch-dependent-file-in-usr-share), and Debian's qemu-system-data
    overrides both for usr/share/qemu. Neither the dynamic loader nor
    dpkg-shlibdeps searches /usr/share for libraries.
  * An ELF file of the host's machine is still traced wherever it is, an
    unreadable one is still unusable, and a foreign one in a library,
    module or program directory, or in an architecture-dependent package,
    still fails the machine check.
  * A static-pie program (ET_DYN with DF_1_PIE, no PT_INTERP, no NEEDED)
    is static to the gate, as it is to file(1), glibc's ldd and Lintian's
    statically-linked-binary (#1068304). Debian builds qemu-user's 33
    programs so and the package depends on no libc, so the gate asked for
    a loader they never name ("qualified interpreter missing:
    /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2"); the published +stereo3d6 failed as a
    predecessor once qemu-system-data passed. A shared library without
    NEEDED has no DF_1_PIE and is still traced.
  * With both, the gate passes the 20 archives of QEMU
    1:11.1.2+ds-3+stereo3d7+lc1 against the published +stereo3d6.
  * dpkg-stereo, the renderer, projection, restore, --resolve and the dpkg
    hooks are 0.1.22's, unchanged; the renderer identity names lean
    0.1.22.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.22 (2026-11-01)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * A conffile in its package's own bytes is never called changed by the
    person (ORDER 325). dpkg-stereo read every package's Conffiles entries
    into one map, so a conffile that moved kept the entry of the package it
    left whenever that package came later in dpkg's status file: on a fresh
    install Debian's binutils-common records /etc/gprofng.rc as "newconffile
    remove-on-upgrade" (deb-conffiles(5)) while binutils owns the file, and
    --verify said "EDITED /etc/gprofng.rc (changed by the person: left as
    they made it)" of Debian's bytes. Each path is now judged by the
    conffiles and md5sums of the package whose file list holds it, as dpkg
    does ("The 'conffiles' data in the status file is ignored when a
    package is not also listed in the file ownership database as having
    that file", src/main/unpack.c; dpkg --verify checks each package's own
    files, src/main/verify.c). The Conffiles field is read as dpkg reads it
    (lib/dpkg/fields.c): its obsolete and remove-on-upgrade flags, and paths
    with spaces.
  * A path a package's conffiles list twice is judged by its first entry,
    the only one dpkg configures (src/main/configure.c): the second keeps
    "newconffile" or an older version's hash. On an installed edition that
    made /etc/default/nis (nis 4.5) and /etc/ld.so.conf.d/gds-13-4.conf
    (libcufile-13-4) EDITED in their package's bytes, as mime-support's
    obsolete entry did to the /etc/mime.types media-types owns.
  * A file taken over through Replaces is judged by its new owner's
    md5sums: dpkg takes the path out of the old package's file list and
    leaves the old package's md5sums line behind.
  * The upgrade corrects what 0.1.21 and older recorded: at the end of the
    dpkg run that installs it, the post-invoke hook checks each conffile
    recorded as changed by the person once against its own package's hash
    (marker conffile-owner-v1). Those in their package's bytes are examined
    again like any other file; a file the person changed stays EDITED,
    left as they made it.
  * The lean toolkit is 0.1.21's, unchanged, and so is the renderer
    identity.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.21 (2026-11-01)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * The lean toolkit's link gate (archive.py, carried here with the rest of
    the toolkit; dpkg-stereo itself does not run it) reads a flat source:
    a suite ending in "/" is an exact path with no components, as
    sources.list(5) says and as NVIDIA publishes its CUDA repository. A
    source's optional architectures list limits it as sources.list's
    Architectures option does (ORDER 329).
  * The gate applies the APT preferences that the supplied edition packages
    ship directly in /etc/apt/preferences.d (named by their conffiles and
    md5sums control members), after its own exact pins, as an installed
    edition has them before its other packages. With the CUDA repository
    among its archives, nvidia-kernel-open-dkms-stereo3d
    615.71.09-2+stereo3d7+lc1 stopped the publication of 1 November
    (firmware-nvidia-gsp (= 615.71.09-2): CUDA's newer 615.71.10-1 is APT's
    candidate and APT takes only the candidate for an exact dependency);
    sparky-stereo-nvidia-driver-lock's pin makes 615.71.09-2 the candidate
    in the gate, as on an installed edition. Each case records the files
    it applied in preferences.json and resolution.json.
  * The renderer, projection, restore, --resolve and the dpkg hooks are
    0.1.20's (ORDER 334, below), unchanged; 0.1.20 was never published, so
    an upgrade from 0.1.19 makes 0.1.20's C and C++ migration. The renderer
    identity names lean 0.1.21.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.20 (2026-11-01)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * C and C++ lose their comment-only lines completely, as every other
    language does (Daniel's design, ORDER 334): no #line, and no empty line
    where a comment was; the authored empty lines stay. Their lean line
    numbers differ from the authored ones, so __LINE__, assert messages,
    diagnostics and debug line tables name lean lines. A comment that ended
    a backslash continuation takes that backslash with it. The renderer
    identity is lean 0.1.20 (collapse-blank,pointer,c-drop-lines).
  * dpkg-stereo --resolve PATH:LINE[:COL] finds the authored line by the lean
    line's content in the authored file (the HR companion or the pristine
    copy): the line's code, its comments and blanks aside; where the same
    content occurs more than once, the neighbouring lines pick the right
    one. It reads the forms older engines wrote too. --resolve-render keeps
    the previous path: render the authored text again, refuse a lean file
    that is not that render, and read the render's own line map.
  * The GCC check compares the tokens and directives (-fpreprocessed) and the
    full preprocessing with __LINE__ pinned; it names a file whose own code
    expands __LINE__ or calls __builtin_LINE.
  * The postinst's --repair-consumers projects again, once, every C and C++
    file an older engine projected locally (#line, or every line kept), from
    its exact original; a file the person edited stays theirs, and a file
    whose original is nowhere waits as before.
  * Measured with ORDER 303's method (instructions:u): against Debian's
    libstdc++ headers, preprocessing prog.cc costs 2.9% fewer instructions
    than the authored headers (0.1.19: 1.9% fewer; 0.1.15: 21.5% more), and
    -fsyntax-only on all of them 0.63% fewer; against the kernel 7.3.1
    headers, the hid-tmff2 module preprocesses with 0.78% fewer and compiles
    with 0.52% fewer. Objects without debug information stay the same,
    except where an enabled assertion passes a header's line
    (_GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS: std::__glibcxx_assert_fail gets the lean line).

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.19 (2026-10-31)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * The lean toolkit's link gate (archive.py, carried here with the rest of
    the toolkit; dpkg-stereo itself does not run it) asks APT for all the
    archives a case downloaded in one bounded apt-cache show call, and
    reuses a record already read for the same identity and SHA-256 under
    the same local index for the rest of the run (ORDER 320). The first
    case of round 316 asked APT 1,047 times, one package at a time; now 2
    calls, and a later case of the same edition snapshot 0 or 1. For 600
    identities of Debian forky's index, one call took 0.47 s and 600 single
    calls 278 s, with the same records.
  * Each record is matched to its own Package, Version and Architecture:
    with several arguments apt-cache skips an absent one silently (exit 0),
    and retries an absent name containing '.' as an unanchored regular
    expression that prints other packages. The checks are unchanged
    (SHA256, Size, Depends, Pre-Depends, Provides, Conflicts, Breaks,
    Multi-Arch). A download without a record of its identity is refused
    by name, also where 0.1.18 said "differs" (a name APT knows, without
    that version); a disk floor stays a space refusal.
  * Projection, restore and the dpkg hooks are the same code as in 0.1.18;
    the renderer identity names lean 0.1.19.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.18 (2026-10-31)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * The C and C++ migration is paid once (ORDER 315, from what ORDER 310
    measured with 0.1.17). restore() tests the names it is given as a set:
    with the list the migration passed, its filter took 68.9 s on the
    station's 90,217 records and 47,360 C and C++ names (55.1 s on a
    synthetic install of that size; 0.55 s now).
  * A C or C++ file whose package version neither APT's cache nor its
    lists hold waits: its version is remembered with the lists and the
    cache as they were when it was tried, and it is tried again only when
    its package, the lists or the cache change. With 38 such versions
    (13,239 files, the station's), every dpkg run read all records and made
    38 failing downloads, about 80 s a run on the station (2.4 s and 38
    attempts a run on the synthetic install); now a dpkg run reads one
    small table (0.001 s, no attempt). One apt-cache madison call says
    which versions the lists still offer, so no download is tried for one
    they lack. --repair-consumers tries them all now; --verify names them
    (PENDING), and they are not problems: their line numbers are the
    authored ones.
  * Progress lines on the terminal apt shows: before the long restore and
    projection, every 1,000 files, and at least once a minute as files are
    done (in the large proof, 1,000 heavy headers took 8 minutes); standard
    output is line-buffered.
  * dpkg's database is read with its journal (/var/lib/dpkg/updates), as
    dpkg reads it. Inside a dpkg run the status file alone is out of date:
    during a first install it listed 81 packages as installed where dpkg's
    database (dpkg-query) listed 154, and the postinst recorded those 81,
    without libstdc++-16-dev. The hooks are not active in the run
    that installs this package (their file is a conffile, in place only at
    its configure), so a trigger (interest-noawait) projects what the run
    configures after it, at the end of the run.
  * A migration inside a dpkg hook leaves packages.tsv as it was, so the
    packages that same run changed are still projected by post-invoke
    (0.1.17 marked them all as seen when it projected again).
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.17 (2026-10-31)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * C and C++ keep each removed comment's line breaks instead of writing #line
    after every removed run: every authored line keeps its number, and GCC no
    longer pays for the directives (ORDER 303; with #line, preprocessing
    Debian's libstdc++ headers took 24.8% more instructions than the
    originals). The pointer takes a line a removed comment emptied.
  * The GCC check also compares where every logical line starts, the lines
    of every fully preprocessed token, and refuses any added line control.
  * On upgrade, --repair-consumers and the dpkg hooks project again the C and
    C++ files an older renderer projected with #line: exact originals first,
    files the person changed left as they are; a file whose original is
    missing keeps its old form and is the only one tried again.
  * --resolve maps C and C++ columns through the renderer's map.
  * The renderer identity names the form and carries its code's digest
    (lean 0.1.17); it changes whenever the renderer's code does.
